course: dictionary
lesson: 2124
title: Convict: a word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Convict can be a verb (a doing word) or a noun (a naming word). As a verb, to convict someone means a court decides officially that they are guilty of a crime. As a noun, a convict is the person who has been found guilty and sent to prison. Context tells you which one you are reading.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Why be a convict?» has a before the word, so it is a noun, and it means a person in prison for a crime. «Courts convict criminals» has a verb job: the courts decide the criminals are guilty. Ask yourself: is it a person, or is it an action?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a longer phrase. «An escaped convict» means a prisoner who got out of prison without permission. If your swapped sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Convict is a verb when a court decides someone is guilty, and a noun when it is the guilty person.
fact: «Convicted of» is a phrase that goes with a crime, such as «convicted of theft».
fact: In 1788 the First Fleet of eleven ships brought British convicts and soldiers to Sydney Cove to start a prison colony.
fact: From 1788 to 1868 Britain sent about 160,000 convicts to Australia on ships, a punishment called transportation.
exercise: «In 1788 the First Fleet of eleven ships brought British convicts and soldiers to Sydney Cove.» What is a convict here? | A sailor who sails the sea for fun | A captain who steers the ship | A soldier who guards the harbour | *A person found guilty of a crime and sent to prison
exercise: «Courts convict criminals.» What does convict mean in this sentence? | To run away from the police | To let someone out of prison | To pay for a crime | *To decide officially that someone is guilty
exercise: «An escaped convict» is a prisoner who | was given a prize for good behaviour | was found innocent in court | was sent to Australia on a ship | *got out of prison without permission
test: «Why be a convict?» What is a convict? | A judge who decides what is fair | A teacher of history | *A person found guilty and sent to prison | A ship that sails to Sydney
test: From 1788 to 1868, about how many convicts did Britain send to Australia? | about 1,600 | *about 160,000 | about 16 million | about 1,060
exam: What is the difference between to convict someone and a convict? | To convict is to pay a fine, and a convict is a fine | To convict is to escape, and a convict is a judge | There is no difference, they mean the same | *To convict is the court's decision that someone is guilty, and a convict is that guilty person sent to prison
